1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,584
The school links spinal protection to pediatric trauma and follows it
with Sunday chest interventions. This lesson teaches a connected
assessment rather than treating a collar and a chest seal as separate
tricks.

2
00:00:13,584 --> 00:00:23,928
Begin with the patient and the rule eligibility. A negative answer
produced by a tool outside its intended population is not reliable
reassurance.

3
00:00:23,928 --> 00:00:35,376
Then distinguish the chest problems: air, blood, injured lung and
impaired cardiac filling can produce overlapping signs but need
different definitive treatments.

4
00:00:35,376 --> 00:00:46,488
The narrated practical prepares recognition, communication and
supervised demonstration. It does not expand independent PCP authority
for invasive chest procedures.

5
00:00:46,488 --> 00:00:59,328
The saved school discussion describes a twelve-year-old with arm
tingling after a vehicle impact. It also names Canadian C-Spine or
NEXUS. That wording needs an explicit age qualification.

6
00:00:59,328 --> 00:01:12,072
The original Canadian rule studied alert stable adults starting at
sixteen; the local handbook provides a separate under-sixteen pathway.
Do not clear the school child by simply running an adult rule.

7
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:26,160
For rehearsal, use a new thirteen-year-old cyclist with new bilateral
hand tingling. Assess immediate threats, symptom onset and
neurological function, and protect movement while arranging
appropriate support.

8
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:37,800
Do not ask the symptomatic child to rotate the neck to pass a
checklist. New symptoms warrant a cautious pediatric plan even when
the first brief motor test appears normal.

9
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:49,992
A vertebral injury and cord injury are related but not identical. A
fracture may have no initial deficit, and cord dysfunction may occur
without an obvious fracture on initial imaging.

10
00:01:49,992 --> 00:02:02,712
The primary insult occurred during the event. Your useful influence
includes preventing additional unnecessary movement and avoiding
hypoxemia, inadequate perfusion and temperature loss.

11
00:02:02,712 --> 00:02:12,576
A collar is therefore only one possible component of protection. It
cannot repair the injury or compensate for an unrecognized airway or
shock problem.

12
00:02:12,576 --> 00:02:24,912
Use a patient-specific plan that balances benefit and harm. Do not
force an intolerable position or delay lifesaving treatment to make
the patient look like an ideal textbook photograph.

13
00:02:24,912 --> 00:02:36,624
Ask what changed at the event and whether it is changing now. Record
the patient words accurately and distinguish reported tingling from an
objectively demonstrated sensory deficit.

14
00:02:36,624 --> 00:02:48,480
Assess bilateral motor and sensory function using safe tasks. Do not
force an injured joint through movement to complete a test; document
why a component could not be assessed.

15
00:02:48,480 --> 00:03:02,280
A brief normal examination does not exclude every cord or mechanically
unstable injury. It establishes a baseline for comparison and
contributes to, rather than replaces, the decision pathway.

16
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:14,688
Recheck after movement, device application and clinical change. New
weakness or altered sensation needs immediate communication and
reassessment of the patient and the movement plan.

17
00:03:14,688 --> 00:03:26,160
Spinal shock is a neurological state after injury, often with reduced
tone and reflexes below the lesion. The word shock here does not
itself mean low circulating volume.

18
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:39,384
Neurogenic shock concerns circulation after sympathetic control is
disrupted. Hypotension with relative bradycardia and warm skin can
support it, but hemorrhage can coexist and must still be sought.

19
00:03:39,384 --> 00:03:53,952
SCIWORA reminds you that neurological injury may not appear as a
fracture on radiographs or CT. Persistent neurological symptoms
require hospital evaluation; tingling alone is not a field diagnosis
of that syndrome.

20
00:03:53,952 --> 00:04:05,232
Do not call an early apparent complete deficit permanently
irreversible. Acute examination limits, swelling and shock can
complicate classification and prognosis.

21
00:04:05,232 --> 00:04:18,576
For an eligible adult, the local modified NEXUS features are midline
tenderness, altered consciousness, a new focal neurological deficit,
intoxication and a major distracting injury.

22
00:04:18,576 --> 00:04:32,688
A positive feature or an unreliable assessment is not a negative
screen. Establish that the patient can understand, report and
participate; do not guess that a difficult history means no pain.

23
00:04:32,688 --> 00:04:46,800
The negative branch requires all five features to be absent. The adult
resource still flags older age, existing spinal disease and
thoracolumbar concerns, so cervical questions do not finish every
movement decision.

24
00:04:46,800 --> 00:05:00,336
Learn one actual pathway and document it. Do not shop between rules
until one produces the no-restriction answer you wanted, or mix part
of NEXUS with part of Canadian C-Spine into a new unvalidated rule.

25
00:05:00,336 --> 00:05:13,128
The Canadian C-Spine Rule is a staged radiography decision rule for
its intended alert stable adult population. It is not a general neck
exercise for unstable patients or symptomatic children.

26
00:05:13,128 --> 00:05:26,952
Its high-risk features include age sixty-five or older, a dangerous
mechanism and paresthesias in the extremities. A high-risk feature
leads to the imaging branch, not to an active rotation test.

27
00:05:26,952 --> 00:05:42,120
The dangerous-mechanism examples include a fall around one metre or
five stairs, axial load, high-speed collision, rollover or ejection,
motorized recreational vehicle and bicycle collision. Use the actual
rule wording.

28
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:53,976
Do not confuse that cervical-rule fall threshold with the separate
three-metre thoracolumbar caution in the local SMR resource. Similar
numbers in different tools have different purposes.

29
00:05:53,976 --> 00:06:07,368
If no high-risk feature is present, the rule asks whether an
applicable low-risk factor permits safe range-of-motion assessment.
Examples include a simple rear-end collision or sitting in the
emergency department.

30
00:06:07,368 --> 00:06:18,624
Other listed factors are having walked at any time, delayed neck-pain
onset or no midline tenderness. These are not five mandatory negative
findings copied from NEXUS.

31
00:06:18,624 --> 00:06:31,608
Simple rear-end has exclusions such as being pushed into oncoming
traffic, impact by a large bus or truck, rollover or high-speed
impact. Do not shorten every rear collision to simple.

32
00:06:31,608 --> 00:06:43,776
If no qualifying low-risk factor permits assessment, the rule goes to
imaging. A patient who walked can still have an injury; the factor is
a step in the sequence, not a guarantee.

33
00:06:43,776 --> 00:06:56,616
Only after the earlier branches allow it does the rule assess active
rotation forty-five degrees left and right. The patient performs the
movement; you do not force the neck through a passive range.

34
00:06:56,616 --> 00:07:08,784
Inability to perform the required movement follows the imaging branch.
Do not keep trying, manipulate through pain or reinterpret a partial
turn as completion to obtain a preferred result.

35
00:07:08,784 --> 00:07:20,376
The original result concerns the need for imaging in its setting.
Operational decisions about field SMR still require the actual local
pathway, assessment and documentation.

36
00:07:20,376 --> 00:07:31,848
For the school child with arm tingling, this branch is not the
appropriate test. Protect the patient and follow the pediatric plan
rather than demonstrating adult-rule neck motion.

37
00:07:31,848 --> 00:07:46,008
Consider a new thirty-eight-year-old who is alert and stable after a
reported simple rear impact. Before using a rule, verify what actually
happened and whether any exclusion or high-risk feature exists.

38
00:07:46,008 --> 00:07:57,624
Do not infer no paresthesia or no midline tenderness from the fact
that the patient is sitting and talking. Ask and examine the relevant
features with a reliable communication plan.

39
00:07:57,624 --> 00:08:09,816
Explain the decision in order. If any necessary earlier step is
uncertain, do not manufacture a final negative result; use the
appropriate protective and consultation approach.

40
00:08:09,816 --> 00:08:22,224
Document which pathway was used, the relevant positives and negatives,
and what movement plan followed. A note saying cleared by rule without
the actual evidence is weak handover information.

41
00:08:22,224 --> 00:08:33,072
The cervical tools do not exclude thoracic or lumbar injury. A patient
with no neck pain can still have important back injury after axial
loading or a significant fall.

42
00:08:33,072 --> 00:08:45,192
The local resource flags findings such as a fall above three metres,
high-speed collision or rollover, pre-existing spinal disease and new
back deformity, bruising or midline tenderness.

43
00:08:45,192 --> 00:08:54,240
Do not sit the patient up or raise the head of the stretcher merely
because a cervical question set was negative when thoracolumbar injury
remains a concern.

44
00:08:54,240 --> 00:09:07,632
Older age, osteoporosis or an abnormal baseline spine can make modest
forces important and idealized flat positioning harmful. Use supported
patient-specific alignment and clinical advice.

45
00:09:07,632 --> 00:09:22,320
The viewed local pediatric matrix considers multitrauma, major
mechanism with altered consciousness and acute neurological deficit.
Its second page adds torticollis, midline pain and predisposing
conditions.

46
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:35,616
It does not provide the adult simple-SMR branch. That is not an
instruction to force a child into an ill-fitting collar; the resource
explicitly recognizes tolerance and the harms of coercive positioning.

47
00:09:35,616 --> 00:09:48,840
Use appropriate manual support, padding and approved alternatives with
a calm explanation. Account for the relatively large head and avoid
creating flexion through a poorly chosen surface or pad.

48
00:09:48,840 --> 00:10:01,728
New reported tingling still needs a protective assessment even if no
focal deficit is yet proven. Do not use the diagram as permission to
ignore symptoms that do not fit a hurried checkbox.

49
00:10:01,728 --> 00:10:15,072
Choose a movement method for the patient and scene, not automatically
a log roll for every examination. A suspected unstable pelvis makes
unnecessary rolling particularly undesirable.

50
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:28,272
Name a leader, prepare the route and receiving surface, assign
sufficient trained hands and explain the command sequence. Protect
tubing, bleeding-control devices and the patient during the move.

51
00:10:28,272 --> 00:10:43,032
Do not force neutral alignment through pain, resistance or worsening
neurological symptoms. The current local material does not tape the
head, and its extrication devices are not automatic prolonged
transport surfaces.

52
00:10:43,032 --> 00:10:56,232
Immediately after movement, repeat the primary assessment and relevant
neurological findings. A patient packaged neatly can still have an
obstructed airway, impaired breathing or a displaced binder.

53
00:10:56,232 --> 00:11:08,448
Assess for obstruction, blood, vomiting, voice change and ineffective
ventilation. A high cervical cord injury can weaken breathing without
an obvious obstructed mouth.

54
00:11:08,448 --> 00:11:21,456
If a collar obstructs necessary access, obtain trained assistance and
use the appropriate supported airway method. Do not preserve an
accessory at the expense of effective oxygenation and ventilation.

55
00:11:21,456 --> 00:11:34,512
Choose the least invasive effective strategy within authority and
escalate when it fails. An isolated GCS total is not an automatic
independent instruction for intubation or sedative use.

56
00:11:34,512 --> 00:11:47,304
When paralysis limits motor scoring, document that confounder using
the structured GCS approach. Do not invent a standard score from a
substitute movement simply to fill every box.

57
00:11:47,304 --> 00:11:59,040
Hypotension after trauma needs a search for hemorrhage and other
causes even when spinal injury is suspected. Do not assume warm skin
or a slow pulse proves that bleeding is absent.

58
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:21,456
H05 gives a systolic pressure target above one hundred twenty with
clear neurological deficit in its explanatory body, while its PCP
bullet says suspected or confirmed spinal cord injury. Keep both
wordings visible rather than narrowing the bullet silently. This is
not the same goal as selected permissive hypotension for uncontrolled
hemorrhage.

59
00:12:21,456 --> 00:12:35,472
If competing injuries create conflicting priorities, identify them and
obtain the appropriate clinical support. Do not average incompatible
numbers or automatically give large volumes until every target is
reached.

60
00:12:35,472 --> 00:12:48,960
Continue measured reassessment of breathing and perfusion with each
intervention. Clinical deterioration is a reason to revisit the
mechanism and plan, not merely to repeat the same fluid action.

61
00:12:48,960 --> 00:13:04,656
Record the mechanism and original position, symptom onset and why the
examination was or was not reliable. An interpreter or adapted
communication plan can improve assessment; language difference is not
proof of incapacity.

62
00:13:04,656 --> 00:13:15,504
Describe the actual neurological findings and any untestable
component. Report new change immediately rather than waiting until
arrival to complete the written form.

63
00:13:15,504 --> 00:13:26,352
Explain the movement pathway, device and any patient-specific
modification. Document why an alternative to an intolerable collar or
flat position was chosen.

64
00:13:26,352 --> 00:13:38,592
A statement such as hand tingling unchanged, motor examination limited
by wrist injury is more accurate than calling the examination fully
normal or diagnosing a permanent cord lesion.

65
00:13:38,592 --> 00:13:51,000
In a new original chest case, a forty-five-year-old has a sharp-object
injury with painful breathing and a small bubbling wound on the left
chest. The patient is initially alert.

66
00:13:51,000 --> 00:14:03,024
During transfer, breathing becomes more difficult, the left side is
quieter and pressure falls. Do not declare the patient safe because
the first observation set was acceptable.

67
00:14:03,024 --> 00:14:15,336
Reassess airway, ventilation, oxygenation and circulation together.
Check any applied dressing or seal and consider pleural air, blood and
associated injury.

68
00:14:15,336 --> 00:14:28,152
State the immediate authorized support, the advanced resource request
and the evolving handover. A likely need for decompression does not by
itself authorize every crew member to puncture the chest.

69
00:14:28,152 --> 00:14:41,664
Expose the chest enough to identify significant wounds and abnormal
movement, then preserve warmth. Look for a second wound without
probing a tract or disturbing an impaled object unnecessarily.

70
00:14:41,664 --> 00:14:54,336
Assess rate, depth, effort and effective chest movement. Compare both
sides and listen in relevant areas; a fast rate alone does not tell
you whether ventilation is adequate.

71
00:14:54,336 --> 00:15:06,696
Gentle palpation may reveal tenderness, instability or subcutaneous
air. Do not spend precious time eliciting a perfect percussion note
while the patient is visibly deteriorating.

72
00:15:06,696 --> 00:15:17,808
Integrate the chest findings with perfusion and mental status. Repeat
after movement and treatment because the relevant pattern may only
become clear over time.

73
00:15:17,808 --> 00:15:28,704
A pneumothorax is air in the pleural space that impairs expansion of
the affected lung. Simple does not mean harmless; the injury can
enlarge or evolve.

74
00:15:28,704 --> 00:15:40,584
When pressure rises enough to interfere with cardiac filling and
circulation, tension physiology develops. The danger is both
inadequate breathing and reduced useful cardiac output.

75
00:15:40,584 --> 00:15:53,136
A hemothorax contains blood, producing loss from the circulation as
well as space occupied in the chest. Blood and air can coexist, so the
mechanisms need not be mutually exclusive.

76
00:15:53,136 --> 00:16:04,344
A pulmonary contusion injures the gas-exchange tissue itself and can
worsen after an initially mild examination. No single device addresses
all four problems.

77
00:16:04,344 --> 00:16:15,696
An open pneumothorax communicates through the chest wall. It may suck,
bubble or be quiet; absence of a memorable sound does not exclude a
serious wound.

78
00:16:15,696 --> 00:16:24,936
Do not use a remembered wound-size cutoff as the decision to begin
assessment. A small external defect can accompany substantial internal
injury.

79
00:16:24,936 --> 00:16:36,720
Inspect safely for additional wounds and stabilize an impaled object
as appropriate. Do not pack or probe the chest, and do not remove an
object merely to fit a dressing.

80
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:48,480
Choose the applicable wound strategy and keep watching the patient. A
wound that looks covered can still be associated with ongoing pleural
leakage, bleeding or worsening circulation.

81
00:16:48,480 --> 00:17:01,560
There is a real source conflict. Current H06 essentials prefer a
commercial vented seal or leaving an open wound open, while its FR
subsection still describes a three-sided dressing.

82
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:13,656
The assigned provincial examination snapshot also lists a three-sided
dressing or commercial chest seal. This is not evidence that every
improvised three-sided flap reliably vents.

83
00:17:13,656 --> 00:17:26,208
For a school station, establish which actual method and device the
instructor requires. For patient care, use the confirmed operational
procedure and seek support when instructions conflict.

84
00:17:26,208 --> 00:17:38,400
Do not create a hybrid that combines the advantages claimed by each
source while hiding its limitations. The non-negotiable task is
immediate patient reassessment and response to deterioration.

85
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:51,960
Use the real chest-seal product and its preparation instructions.
Adhesion, skin moisture, blood and positioning can affect performance;
do not assume every package uses the same method.

86
00:17:51,960 --> 00:18:03,600
Apply without obstructing an intended vent and reassess both breathing
and circulation. Check the device again after movement, blankets or
equipment have been rearranged.

87
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:15,432
If distress or perfusion worsens after sealing, assess for tension and
check or lift the seal as indicated by the applicable procedure while
requesting the appropriate advanced response.

88
00:18:15,432 --> 00:18:27,480
Do not declare success because the adhesive stayed on. The clinical
endpoint is an improving or supported patient, with recognition that
internal air leakage or another injury may remain.

89
00:18:27,480 --> 00:18:38,136
Continuing air entry can raise pleural pressure and impair the
affected lung. That creates worsening respiratory distress and
ineffective gas exchange.

90
00:18:38,136 --> 00:18:48,840
The same pressure can impede venous return and cardiac filling,
reducing output. This explains why a chest problem can present as
rapidly worsening shock.

91
00:18:48,840 --> 00:19:01,992
Look for progressive distress, abnormal movement, unilateral reduced
air entry and hemodynamic compromise in the actual context. Tension
and hemorrhage may occur together.

92
00:19:01,992 --> 00:19:13,200
A likely need for urgent decompression demands prompt escalation while
you continue authorized support. Waiting for an ideal complete set of
classic signs can be dangerous.

93
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:23,400
Tracheal deviation is a possible late finding, not a necessary early
trigger. Do not wait to see the windpipe shift in a patient already
deteriorating.

94
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:33,528
Neck veins may not be visibly distended when there is concurrent
volume loss. A flat neck vein therefore does not exclude a pressure-
related chest threat.

95
00:19:33,528 --> 00:19:46,536
Percussion and auscultation are affected by noise, body habitus and
technique. Use their findings with the rest of the assessment, not as
absolute proof that air or blood must be absent.

96
00:19:46,536 --> 00:19:58,896
A patient who was initially stable can worsen after movement or
positive pressure. Reassessment and a communicated failure plan are
essential even after a technically tidy intervention.

97
00:19:58,896 --> 00:20:11,808
If the patient is apneic or not ventilating effectively, necessary
ventilation must continue. Concern about a pneumothorax is not
permission to let the patient remain unventilated.

98
00:20:11,808 --> 00:20:24,768
Use the trained effective technique, avoid excessive pressure and
volume and watch chest rise and response. Request advanced assistance
when a chest threat makes ventilation increasingly difficult.

99
00:20:24,768 --> 00:20:36,984
CPAP or an added PEEP valve is not automatically the next step for
every breathless trauma patient. The reviewed local procedures have
specific pneumothorax and arrest exclusions.

100
00:20:36,984 --> 00:20:48,960
Nitrous oxide is also inappropriate in the suspected pneumothorax
context. Choose analgesia through the actual patient-specific pathway
while preserving breathing and perfusion.

101
00:20:48,960 --> 00:21:00,432
The Week Four school outline explicitly teaches needle thoracostomy
and chest seals. That establishes a learning objective, not an
automatic independent clinical endorsement.

102
00:21:00,432 --> 00:21:12,792
The current H06 lists needle decompression for ACP and CCP, not
independent PCP care. Its newer finger-thoracostomy addition is a
different CCP procedure.

103
00:21:12,792 --> 00:21:25,080
A student can learn why decompression works, identify equipment and
demonstrate under the instructor supervision required by the program.
This does not change licensing or operational authorization.

104
00:21:25,080 --> 00:21:36,600
State your actual role during a case: recognize deterioration, provide
authorized support, request an appropriate clinician and prepare a
safe handover or supervised station.

105
00:21:36,600 --> 00:21:49,704
For supervised study, the January Twenty Twenty-Six PR21 identifies
the second interspace at the midclavicular line, just above the third
rib, using the sternal angle to find the second rib.

106
00:21:49,704 --> 00:22:02,280
It also describes the fourth or fifth interspace at the midaxillary
line, superior to the fifth or sixth rib respectively. An interspace
and the number of the rib below it are not the same label.

107
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:15,120
Use the superior border of the lower rib to avoid the neurovascular
structures beneath the rib above. Do not use the nipple as the sole
landmark or assume every body has the same chest-wall thickness.

108
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:27,624
This is anatomy preparation on the approved model, not a patient
puncture instruction for an unauthorized provider. Confirm the actual
device, body habitus and instructor requirements.

109
00:22:27,624 --> 00:22:39,240
The current PDF illustrates a BD twelve-gauge three-inch catheter and
a Turkel eight-French, twelve-gauge three-and-a-half-inch assembly.
They are examples with different construction.

110
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:53,040
The Turkel has a contact indicator and a three-way stopcock that
starts closed. The BD example does not require adding a three-way
stopcock. Device opening is therefore not a universal identical step.

111
00:22:53,040 --> 00:23:05,976
Do not combine the old textbook improvised glove valve, one pictured
catheter and another device cap into a homemade hybrid. Follow the
actual authorized assembly and its instructions.

112
00:23:05,976 --> 00:23:19,104
The school may use another training model. Recognize parts, opening
state and sharps handling on that model before the supervised
demonstration, rather than claiming the PDF proves school equipment
identity.

113
00:23:19,104 --> 00:23:30,744
In the supervised model sequence, first confirm the indication, role,
equipment and landmark, then use the prescribed aseptic preparation
and controlled handling.

114
00:23:30,744 --> 00:23:43,920
The current procedure stops needle advancement after pleural entry,
then stabilizes the assembly while the sharp is removed and the
flexible catheter is advanced. Do not drive a metal needle to the hub
by rote.

115
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:55,872
Dispose of the sharp safely and open the actual device as required. A
closed stopcock can prevent the intended relief; an improvised extra
valve is not automatically useful.

116
00:23:55,872 --> 00:24:08,712
Then reassess chest movement, air entry and hemodynamics. The observed
response and ongoing patency matter more than having completed the
motion or heard an initial air sound.

117
00:24:08,712 --> 00:24:19,728
A catheter may not enter the pleural space or may become blocked,
kinked or displaced. An initial air release is not proof of durable
clinical success.

118
00:24:19,728 --> 00:24:32,112
The authorized clinician must reassess the diagnosis and physiology.
Mainstem intubation can produce unilateral findings, and hemorrhage or
tamponade can coexist with a pneumothorax.

119
00:24:32,112 --> 00:24:47,592
An unnecessary or misplaced puncture can cause another pneumothorax,
injure structures or produce bleeding or infection. The absence of
absolute contraindications for a true indication does not mean every
quiet chest needs a needle.

120
00:24:47,592 --> 00:25:00,024
Repeated or alternative decompression belongs to the appropriate
authorized pathway. Continue supportive care and rapid definitive
transfer; a temporary catheter is not the end of the call.

121
00:25:00,024 --> 00:25:10,944
A hemothorax occupies pleural space and removes blood from the useful
circulation. The patient can therefore have both respiratory
compromise and hypovolemic shock.

122
00:25:10,944 --> 00:25:23,736
Blood and air may coexist. Physical signs can overlap and may be
difficult to interpret in the field; do not insist on a perfect
percussion result to recognize a critically injured patient.

123
00:25:23,736 --> 00:25:35,568
A small decompression catheter intended to vent air does not solve
major pleural bleeding. A chest drain and definitive surgical or other
hemorrhage control are different treatments.

124
00:25:35,568 --> 00:25:48,240
Support breathing and perfusion, continue the search for other
bleeding and arrange rapid appropriate trauma care. Report what
remains uncertain rather than promising that one puncture fixed
everything.

125
00:25:48,240 --> 00:26:01,536
A flail segment can move paradoxically, but pain-related splinting or
positive pressure may hide the movement. Do not require an obvious
dramatic segment before addressing inadequate breathing.

126
00:26:01,536 --> 00:26:12,336
The underlying lung contusion and associated injuries can be more
important than the motion itself. Respiratory function can worsen
after the first examination.

127
00:26:12,336 --> 00:26:24,408
Provide indicated analgesia and oxygenation or ventilation support,
with repeated assessment. Do not weigh down or circumferentially bind
the chest to make the movement disappear.

128
00:26:24,408 --> 00:26:38,760
The examination guide mentions manual flail stabilization while Nancy
rejects external stabilization. Confirm the school supervised
technique and avoid inventing a restrictive compromise that impairs
ventilation.

129
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:54,216
Tamponade restricts cardiac filling within the pericardium. The
classic combination of low pressure, distended veins and muffled
sounds may be absent or hard to detect; equal breath sounds do not
definitively identify the diagnosis.

130
00:26:54,216 --> 00:27:08,064
Blunt cardiac injury can cause dysrhythmia or pump dysfunction. Obtain
indicated monitoring and ECG while maintaining trauma priorities,
rather than interpreting a normal first tracing as structural
clearance.

131
00:27:08,064 --> 00:27:21,168
Major deceleration or penetration can injure the great vessels. A
person can initially seem stable; pain, perfusion asymmetry and
subsequent shock need appropriate trauma evaluation.

132
00:27:21,168 --> 00:27:35,280
Diaphragm, esophageal and major-airway injuries can be occult or
delayed. Recognize concerning patterns, support physiology and convey
to definitive care without forcing a precise field diagnosis.

133
00:27:35,280 --> 00:27:45,816
For the pediatric rehearsal, communicate the mechanism, new bilateral
hand symptoms, actual motor and sensory findings and the movement
protection used.

134
00:27:45,816 --> 00:27:57,168
For the chest case, communicate the initial wound and the later change
in breathing, asymmetry and pressure. Distinguish the suspected
pressure threat from confirmed findings.

135
00:27:57,168 --> 00:28:10,368
Include the exact seal or supervised procedure, application time,
effect and any failure or modification. A procedure name without a
response does not tell the receiver what remains urgent.

136
00:28:10,368 --> 00:28:22,632
Finish with the current patient state, support required and explicit
transfer of responsibility. Do not bury a new deficit or failing chest
at the end of a long list of minor injuries.

137
00:28:22,632 --> 00:28:34,392
At the spine station, identify the correct age pathway, assess actual
findings and explain the movement plan. Do not chase a negative adult
rule for the symptomatic child.

138
00:28:34,392 --> 00:28:48,240
At the seal station, identify the real product and demonstrate its
preparation, application, reassessment and failure response. State the
source conflict rather than pretending it does not exist.

139
00:28:48,240 --> 00:29:01,344
At the needle trainer, state the clinical scope boundary, landmark
accurately and follow the actual instructor-approved device sequence.
No live-person puncture is part of this self-study rehearsal.

140
00:29:01,344 --> 00:29:12,816
In the integrated station, a partner introduces deterioration. Repeat
the primary survey, call for the appropriate support and hand over the
evolving patient clearly.

141
00:29:12,816 --> 00:30:01,968
The literal first span finishes urinary assessment before the
musculoskeletal section. Ask about voiding difficulty, pain,
frequency, blood, baseline output and relevant urinary history; a
bladder scan or catheterization is a separate trained/authorized task,
not an automatic response to the history. Distinguish an ileal urinary
conduit from a nephrostomy draining a kidney through the flank.
Observe the actual stoma or insertion site, dressing, tubing and timed
output without pulling, flushing or inserting into an unfamiliar
system. Bowel-derived conduits can produce mucus, so appearance or
smell alone neither proves infection nor makes new fever, flank pain,
bleeding or a large output change normal.

142
00:30:01,968 --> 00:30:48,168
In the musculoskeletal checklist, compare baseline mobility and
permitted activity with posture, assistive devices, skin/bruising,
deformity, swelling and comfortable active movement. Ask about pain,
prior injury, medicines and support needs. Colour, warmth, movement
and sensation, or CWMS, organize observations; normal findings do not
reliably rule out every fracture, evolving compartment problem or
spinal injury. Repeat the appropriate distal neurovascular findings
before and after support or movement. Do not use resistance, a thirty-
degree straight-leg hold or unsupported sitting to challenge a
potentially unstable injured pelvis, spine or limb.

143
00:30:48,168 --> 00:31:33,192
Correct two literal assessment shortcuts. Dorsiflexion brings the
front of the foot toward the shin; plantar flexion points it away. The
source paragraph interchanges the resisted directions, so name and
observe the actual movement rather than memorizing where its hand is
written. Its zero-to-three example assesses motor block associated
with epidural analgesia: zero means no motor block and three means
inability to move knees or ankles. It is not a universal trauma
strength score and cannot be compared directly with another scale
without naming it. Weight-bearing status and a falls-risk tool guide a
plan; neither a score nor willingness to walk overrides an acute
injury restriction.

144
00:31:33,192 --> 00:32:27,504
The assigned mechanical-lift table distinguishes support, not
interchangeable sling names. A universal sling may be fitted in a
chair; the illustrated hammock gives broader head-to-knee support and
cannot simply be removed with the person still seated; a hygiene sling
exposes more area for toileting and gives less, short-duration
support. Real compatibility, size, head control, weight capacity and
training decide selection. The bed-position pages describe supine,
prone, lateral, a side/semi-prone Sims posture and head-up variants.
Their examples use Fowler around forty-five degrees, semi-Fowler
thirty and high Fowler sixty-to-ninety. They are descriptions, not an
order to roll an unstable injury, twist a neck or force every
breathless/shocked patient to one angle.

145
00:32:27,504 --> 00:33:24,552
At the wound-span ending, distinguish mechanism before naming a
dressing. Arterial insufficiency can cause distal tissue ischemia;
venous disease can produce a shallow exuding lower-leg wound with
edema; neuropathy can reduce protective sensation and coexist with
pressure or arterial disease. Appearances overlap, so pulse, location,
pain or a textbook percentage alone cannot settle the cause. Assess
circulation and the whole patient before a compression plan: a
dressing for a venous-looking wound must not become unassessed tight
compression over an ischemic limb. The healing figure shows
overlapping clot, inflammation, new tissue and remodeling, not
guaranteed strength at a calendar date. Literal reading stops at the
introduction to the following healing-factor table; wider named
Chapter4 teaching remains separately identified support.

146
00:33:24,552 --> 00:34:22,752
The literal IV pages compare a short peripheral dressing with a line
that also has a securement device. The shared aim is a clean, dry,
inspectable site without catheter movement: identify the person/line,
prepare an appropriate work area and aseptic materials, stabilize
while removing the old dressing, use the actual antiseptic and allow
it to dry, then apply the compatible dressing/securement and reassess.
A component remaining under a central-line dressing needs the relevant
sterile/no-touch handling; glove removal is not permission to touch
its protected surface. Compare external length with the documented
baseline: a change suggests displacement but an unchanged number does
not prove internal tip position. Do not push an exposed catheter
inward or improvise a central-line dressing procedure from the short-
peripheral checklist.

147
00:34:22,752 --> 00:35:18,960
The last literal span introduces transfusion and stops at the first
three preparation steps, not a completed transfusion competency. Red
cells primarily restore oxygen-carrying capacity; platelets and plasma
address different hemostatic needs, and saline is not equivalent to
replacing lost red cells or clotting components. Allogeneic means
another donor, autologous the person's own blood. Confirm the actual
authorized product, indication, identifiers, compatibility and
individual consent; religion alone does not tell you which components
a person accepts. Correct the source suggestion that irradiation or
leukocyte reduction solves incompatibility: neither replaces
compatibility testing. Irradiation inactivates donor T lymphocytes to
prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, not an ABO
mismatch.

148
00:35:18,960 --> 00:36:10,392
The source reaction list is a warning system, not a requirement for
every feature. Fever/rigors, chest or back pain, dyspnea, hives,
altered pressure, dark urine or other new deterioration may matter
during or after transfusion. Do not wait for a rash to cover twenty-
five percent or for all vital signs to change. Canadian Blood Services
advises stopping a suspected reaction and maintaining vascular access
with saline under the actual response plan, then obtaining urgent
clinical/transfusion-service review. The bedside task remains
assessment and escalation within real training and authority, not
restarting an unknown product or flushing residual blood into the
patient. A new reaction and the original bleeding/illness can coexist.

149
00:36:10,392 --> 00:37:02,880
Explain the literal pre-transfusion preparation as a stop-before-error
chain. Complete the approved preparation before obtaining a
temperature-controlled product; different products have different
storage requirements, so return an unusable or unneeded unit to the
transfusion service rather than storing it in an ordinary
refrigerator. Verify the prescribed component, amount, timing/rate and
special modifications, then actual related medication orders and
previous transfusion/allergy history. Do not give every patient the
source example of acetaminophen, diphenhydramine or furosemide thirty
minutes beforehand: premedication is individualized, not a guarantee
against reactions, and can obscure symptoms. Any identity, order or
product discrepancy means do not proceed until resolved.

150
00:37:02,880 --> 00:37:12,936
You should be able to explain adult NEXUS and Canadian C-Spine without
applying them beyond their intended pathway or using neck motion as
the first examination.

151
00:37:12,936 --> 00:37:26,592
For a child with new neurological symptoms, select a protective
pediatric plan and preserve breathing and perfusion. A collar is not
the whole treatment and normal early bone imaging would not erase a
deficit.

152
00:37:26,592 --> 00:37:37,104
For chest trauma, explain the different mechanisms, the seal failure
plan and why escalating for decompression is distinct from independent
permission to perform it.

153
00:37:37,104 --> 00:37:48,528
Use the two paired Nancy guides and original checks to retrieve the
reasoning. The next lesson covers the assigned burns and temperature
readings without mixing cooling with trauma warming.
